  tenant="Faux Plants and Trees" products=5       t0_rss_kb=2188     with_tenant_rss_kb=2408     peak_serving_rss_kb=2604     peak_growth_kb=196      post_teardown_rss_kb=2732     total_queries_served=81975348  samples: t5s=2604KB t10s=2604KB t15s=2604KB t20s=2604KB
  tenant="Water Filter Pitchers" products=1       t0_rss_kb=2248     with_tenant_rss_kb=2460     peak_serving_rss_kb=2656     peak_growth_kb=196      post_teardown_rss_kb=2784     total_queries_served=196026189  samples: t5s=2652KB t10s=2656KB t15s=2656KB t20s=2656KB

H7 comparison: idle-resident peak growth=244.0 KB (11.2% of t0), max active-resident tenant peak growth=900 KB
  H7 verdict: CONFIRMED -- peak RSS while actually serving is materially higher than H6's immediate snapshot; a genuinely long-running service costs more than the short-lived-process floor
  (note: post-teardown RSS, printed above, is a distinct secondary signal about thread-join/shutdown behavior -- not used for this verdict, since a real long-running service does not tear down its worker pool mid-life)

